# Paths excluded from the distribution tarball produced by release.yml.
# These exist in the template repo for the GAIA team's own workflow but have
# no business on an adopter's machine. Format is tar --exclude-from: one
# literal path per line, comments with `#` are honored.
#
# Anything excluded here is ALSO skipped by `gaia-maintainer release manifest`, so the
# adopter-facing manifest never references files an adopter cannot have.
# Authoritative narrative of what's maintainer-only and why lives in
# `wiki/concepts/Release Workflow.md` (Distribution Boundary section).
#
# Categories below are ordered the same way that page lists them.

# --- 1. Maintainer-only Claude surface ---
# /gaia-release cuts releases of the GAIA template itself; adopters never
# release GAIA. The release-notes skill translates GAIA's CHANGELOG into
# public release notes for the marketing site, a release-time maintainer
# task, so it ships nowhere just like /gaia-release. /health-audit is the
# maintainer-only autonomous audit + auto-heal loop (runbook lives under
# .gaia/cli/health/, already excluded wholesale via category 10). Other
# /gaia-* commands (plan, handoff, pickup, audit) are adopter-useful and
# must NOT be added here. preferred-base.sh is the maintainer's personal
# statusline left-side renderer; adopters get the right-side-only
# gaia-statusline.sh wrapper which delegates left-side rendering to the
# user's own global statusline. The three code-audit-maintainer-* agents are
# the Code Audit Team's maintainer-only members (framework shell + framework
# TypeScript + skills-prose lenses); adopters audit only their own app via
# code-audit-frontend. /distribution-audit is the maintainer's
# ship-or-withhold audit over newly-shipping files; it drives the
# maintainer-only release CLI and has no meaning on an adopter clone.
.claude/commands/gaia-release.md
.claude/commands/health-audit.md
.claude/skills/release-notes
.gaia/statusline/preferred-base.sh
.claude/agents/code-audit-maintainer-shell.md
.claude/agents/code-audit-maintainer-node.md
.claude/agents/code-audit-maintainer-prose.md
.claude/commands/distribution-audit.md
# Maintainer-only Code Audit Team spawn-list optimization; adopters resolve required members via resolve-audit-members.sh and the frontend self-skip is fail-closed on its absence.
.gaia/scripts/resolve-audit-spawn.sh
# The re-spawn measurement instrument exists only to serve the maintainer-only
# spawn oracle above: the shared ledger lib it writes through, the session-start
# prune that bounds the ledger, and the attribution query that reads it. An
# adopter clone never runs the oracle, so it never has a ledger for any of the
# three to act on.
.gaia/scripts/audit-respawn-lib.sh
.gaia/scripts/audit-respawn-prune.sh
.gaia/scripts/audit-respawn-report.sh
# verify-required-checks.sh declares the required_status_checks the GAIA team's own
# branch ruleset must enforce (job names like "Audit CI Tests" that only exist
# on maintainer-only, release-excluded workflows); an adopter clone has no such
# ruleset and none of those jobs, so the script has no adopter surface.
.gaia/scripts/verify-required-checks.sh
# Maintainer-only distribution pre-flight; drives the release-excluded gaia-maintainer binary and would mislead adopter-side agents about a manifest their clone does not have
.claude/hooks/distribution-preflight-check.sh
# The four worktree-program build checks assert invariants that are true of
# GAIA's own source and of nothing else: that each language has exactly one
# main-root resolver (Check A) and no hand-rolled re-derivation of it (Check
# B), that .gaia/state-registry.json covers every state directory, and that no
# shipped source hardcodes a path the registry already answers. An adopter does
# not write GAIA's resolvers or its registry, and every runner of these four is
# already maintainer-only (the .gaia/scripts/tests/ bats suites and
# audit-ci-tests.yml, both release-excluded), so today they ship a check no
# adopter can trigger and could not act on. Withholding them is also what stops
# their scan lists and their sibling-suite citations naming .gaia/cli/src/,
# .gaia/cli/gaia-maintainer and .gaia/scripts/tests/ inside an adopter bundle.
.gaia/scripts/check-main-root-derivation.sh
.gaia/scripts/check-registry-completeness.sh
.gaia/scripts/check-registry-source-literals.sh
.gaia/scripts/check-resolver-singleton.sh
# Task 7.3's check asserts this repository still qualifies for git's merge refusal on the tracked wiki state file; its only runner (audit-ci-tests.yml and its bats suite) is already maintainer-only, and an adopter cannot act on the failure.
.gaia/scripts/check-wiki-state-collision.sh
# The git path-quoting lint guards GAIA's own framework shell and its CI
# workflow YAML against an unquoted `diff --name-only` or `ls-files`, either of
# which lets git C-quote a path the caller then parses. Withheld on the reasoning
# .gaia/tests/shell-lint.sh gives for itself -- adopters run GAIA's bash but
# never author it, so a linter over the framework's own shell has no adopter
# surface -- and shell-lint.sh, its only runner, is already release-excluded
# wholesale via the .gaia/tests entry, so shipping it would ship a check nothing
# on an adopter clone invokes.
.gaia/scripts/lint-git-path-quoting.sh
# The workflow run-interpolation lint guards GAIA's own CI workflow YAML, its
# composite actions, and the adopter workflow templates it renders CI from,
# against a `${{ }}` expression substituted into a `run:` script body. Withheld
# on the same reasoning as the diff-quoting lint above,
# and on that reasoning ALONE: shell-lint.sh, its only runner, is
# release-excluded wholesale via the `.gaia/tests` entry, so shipping this file
# would ship a check nothing on an adopter clone invokes.
#
# Note what is deliberately NOT claimed here, because it is false and would be
# the premise a future maintainer re-litigates this decision from: an adopter
# clone does have a populated scan surface. This file excludes 11 of the 13
# tracked workflows individually rather than excluding the directory, so
# `tests.yml` and `chromatic.yml` ship, and `bundle:adopter` renders further
# workflows into an adopter's `.github/workflows/` from templates the manifest
# ships. The withhold rests on the absent runner, not on an empty surface.
.gaia/scripts/lint-workflow-run-interpolation.sh
# The grep ERE-escape lint guards GAIA's own framework shell, its CI workflow and
# composite-action YAML, and the adopter workflow templates against a
# backslash-escaped letter in a `grep -E` pattern, which BSD grep and GNU grep
# read differently. Withheld on the same reasoning as the two lints above, and on
# that reasoning ALONE: shell-lint.sh, its only runner, is release-excluded
# wholesale via the `.gaia/tests` entry, so shipping this file would ship a check
# nothing on an adopter clone invokes. An adopter clone does have a populated
# scan surface, for the reason the run-interpolation note above spells out, so
# the withhold rests on the absent runner rather than on an empty surface.
.gaia/scripts/lint-grep-ere-escapes.sh
# The errexit status-read lint guards GAIA's own framework shell, its CI workflow
# and composite-action YAML, and the adopter workflow templates against a `$?`
# read that `set -e` has already made unreachable. Withheld on the same reasoning
# as the three lints above, and on that reasoning ALONE: shell-lint.sh, its only
# runner, is release-excluded wholesale via the `.gaia/tests` entry, so shipping
# this file would ship a check nothing on an adopter clone invokes. An adopter
# clone does have a populated scan surface, and the shipped workflow templates
# render into their CI carrying the class's own exposure, so the withhold rests
# on the absent runner rather than on an empty surface.
.gaia/scripts/lint-errexit-status-read.sh
# The shipped-issue-reference lint enforces GAIA's own authoring obligation: a
# `#NNN` on a file GAIA ships must name gaia-react/gaia so an adopter reading it
# is not pointed at their own tracker. Withheld because that obligation is
# GAIA's, not the adopter's -- on an adopter clone a `#NNN` in a file they edited
# means their tracker, and this gate would demand they mislabel it as GAIA's. Its
# runners (the Distribution Audit workflow and its bats suite) are both
# release-excluded, so shipping it would ship a check nothing on an adopter clone
# invokes and whose verdict would be wrong there anyway.
.gaia/scripts/lint-shipped-issue-refs.sh
# Gates GAIA's own pull requests from the release-excluded Audit CI Tests workflow; shipping it would fail an adopter build over code GAIA does not own.
.gaia/scripts/check-script-capabilities.sh
# The static-analysis half of check-script-capabilities.sh, its only caller; it has no adopter surface on its own.
.gaia/scripts/capability-oracle-lib.sh

